## Interpretation

The player-side collection shape is viable for the first implementation pass:

- Real temp characters can be spawned and collected in SIE.
- 50 tagged component transforms can be collected and compacted to 20 slots.
- Runtime Array DI push reaches GPU sim for a single RT system.
- Single-channel RT correctness passed with 20/20 selected and 0 excluded hits.
- The measured Python/editor harness collection loop was about `0.33-0.35 ms` per 50-component collection pass. Treat this as a smoke baseline, not a packaged-game CPU budget.

The current three-independent-system plan is not yet cleared:

- 3-channel runs push all arrays successfully, but only Water writes to its RT.
- Stacking all NiagaraComponents at the same location does not fix it.
- Disabling `set_force_solo` does not fix it.
- This narrows the blocker away from character collection, array push, component culling, or force-solo behavior. The likely remaining area is per-clone Niagara RT/Grid2D binding or template-internal shared state.

## Current Go/No-Go

Go:

- Character-mounted component collection design.
- 50 candidates -> nearest 20 budget shape.
- Single active RT system using the Array DI 3-chain path.
- Profiling harness with Trace.File + ProfileGPU artifacts.

No-go until fixed:

- Three independent cloned Niagara RT painter systems running concurrently as the production default.

Recommended next technical options:

- Prefer one combined Niagara system that owns all required RT/Grid2D outputs, if authoring is practical.
- Or prove independent systems by giving each clone fully unique emitter/module/Grid2D/RT DI namespaces and inspecting compiled bindings.
- Keep per-surface fallback scheduling available: run only the active needed channel first, then add parallel channels after the binding issue is resolved.

